d": 59, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 69.9, "schoolRate": 71.7, "correctAnswer": "A", "errorRate": 30.1, "knowledgePoints": "语法-时态语态", "options": {"A": {"count": 11}, "B": {"count": 2}, "C": {"count": 2}, "D": {"count": 1}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.45}, {"id": 60, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 62.1, "schoolRate": 66.6, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 37.9, "knowledgePoints": "语法-非谓语动词", "options": {"A": {"count": 3}, "B": {"count": 3}, "C": {"count": 3}, "D": {"count": 10}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.54}, {"id": 61, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 82.0, "schoolRate": 81.3, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 18.0, "knowledgePoints": "语法-非谓语动词", "options": {"A": {"count": 1}, "B": {"count": 1}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 13}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.46}, {"id": 62, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 72.9, "schoolRate": 68.0, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 27.1, "knowledgePoints": "语法-非谓语动词", "options": {"A": {"count": 1}, "B": {"count": 1}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 12}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.32}, {"id": 63, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 85.9, "schoolRate": 85.4, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 14.1, "knowledgePoints": "语法-名词性从句", "options": {"A": {"count": 1}, "B": {"count": 1}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 14}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.51}, {"id": 64, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 55.4, "schoolRate": 49.8, "correctAnswer": "A", "errorRate": 44.6, "knowledgePoints": "语法-冠词介词", "options": {"A": {"count": 9}, "B": {"count": 2}, "C": {"count": 3}, "D": {"count": 2}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.41}, {"id": 65, "type": "语法填空", "score": 1.5, "classRate": 70.0, "schoolRate": 72.2, "correctAnswer": "B", "errorRate": 30.0, "knowledgePoints": "语法-时态语态", "options": {"A": {"count": 2}, "B": {"count": 11}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 1}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.52}, {"id": 66, "type": "应用文", "score": 15.0, "classRate": 62.8, "schoolRate": 58.1, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 37.2, "knowledgePoints": "应用文-格式规范", "options": {"A": {"count": 1}, "B": {"count": 1}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 10}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.26}, {"id": 67, "type": "读后续写", "score": 25.0, "classRate": 59.4, "schoolRate": 58.9, "correctAnswer": "D", "errorRate": 40.6, "knowledgePoints": "续写-语言质量", "options": {"A": {"count": 2}, "B": {"count": 2}, "C": {"count": 1}, "D": {"count": 10}}, "unanswered": {"count": 0, "names": []}, "answeredCount": 16, "difficultyDesc": "", "discrimination": 0.29}], "class11": []}, "passages": [{"id": "passage-21", "title": "Passage C — Reading Comprehension", "type": "text", "content": "<p>Early morning, a narrow lane in the old town: a woman waters her tomatoes on a rooftop garden built from recycled barrels. Scenes like this are becoming common in cities around the world, and researchers say urban gardening is doing far more than making grey rooftops look pretty.</p><p>A three-year study of twelve community gardens found that residents who took part reported lower stress levels and a stronger sense of belonging. Working with soil, it seems, slows people down. Neighbours who had never spoken began sharing seeds, tools and recipes, and the gardens quietly turned strangers into friends.</p><p>The benefits reach beyond well-being. Vegetables grown a few metres from the kitchen need no long-distance transport, and rooftop plants cool the rooms below them in summer, reducing the need for air conditioning. One city hall in southern Europe now requires every new public building to dedicate at least a quarter of its roof to greenery.</p><p>Still, urban gardening is no cure-all. Water is scarce in many cities, and soil on old industrial sites can be polluted. The study's authors therefore suggest starting small: a few pots on a balcony, a shared strip of land behind a school. Small gardens, they argue, grow more than food — they grow the habit of caring for the places we live in.</p>", "questions": [21, 22, 23, 24], "questionData": [{"id": 21, "stem": "Why does the author mention the woman watering tomatoes?", "options": ["A. To introduce the topic of urban gardening", "B. To describe life in old towns", "C. To encourage readers to grow tomatoes", "D. To show gardens are expensive to build"]}, {"id": 22, "stem": "According to the study, what changed among the residents?", "options": ["A. They earned more money from selling vegetables", "B. Strangers developed closer relationships", "C. They spent less time at home", "D. They started competing for tools"]}, {"id": 23, "stem": "What practical benefit of rooftop plants is mentioned in Paragraph 3?", "options": ["A. They clean polluted soil", "B. They save water for the city", "C. They reduce the need for air conditioning", "D. They make public buildings taller"]}, {"id": 24, "stem": "What do the study's authors suggest in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A. Every family should build a rooftop garden", "B. Cities should ban air conditioning", "C. Gardening solves all urban problems", "D. Starting with small gardens is wise"]}]}], "mmAnalysis": {"Passage D": {"text": "A popular musician posts a music video or an athlete shares a workout, and suddenly they seem like part of our lives. People like and share these posts, and over time, admiration can start to feel like friendship — people like to think they know celebrities. Psychology Today defines this phenomenon as a parasocial relationship — a one-sided connection that exists only in the fan's mind. First identified in 1956 to describe television viewers feeling closely connected to on-screen figures, this behavior has existed for ages.\n\nToday, social platforms deepen this illusion by offering constant, seemingly real updates on the lives of public figures. However, what audiences actually see is a carefully selected version of reality, edited for perfection rather than truth. Despite this created closeness, young people frequently view celebrities as standards for success. This influence often unconsciously shapes everything from their self-image to their long-term career goals.\n\nThese relationships are not always harmful. For some, especially those who feel left out in society, seeing public figures with similar backgrounds can offer a sense of belonging. These connections can inspire confidence and make people feel less alone. In that sense, they can serve a real and meaningful purpose.\n\nThe danger arises when the line between admiration and imagination disappears. The relationship becomes unhealthy when fans act as if they truly know a celebrity. They might take planned posts as personal messages and develop unrealistic expectations. Furthermore, these feelings now extend to artificial intelligence (AI). Chatbots acting like real friends can produce deep emotional responses, making it harder to tell real human connection from AI.\n\nAdmiration itself is fine. Music, sports and entertainment have long connected communities, but that influence needs clear boundaries. Without them, simply watching a video is easily mistaken for close friendship, and carefully chosen content is mistaken for true connection. Parasocial relationships should remain what they are: distant and one-sided. In a digital age where everything is designed to feel personal, we must remember that it is not.", "roleFns": {}}}, "knowledge": {"class9": {"free": "本卷高频失分点\n1. 语法-非谓语动词（56/60题）：done 与 doing 看逻辑主语关系——口诀：被动完成用 done，主动进行用 doing\n2. 完形-逻辑连词（48/52题）：however 看前后语义相反，therefore 看前后互为条件\n3. 阅读-推理判断（29/33题）：答案必须原文有据，切忌过度推断\n4. 听力-数字信息（8/13题）：price / discount / half off 换算速记", "byType": "", "byGrammar": ""}}}], "appState": {"currentClass": "class9", "currentHomework": "hw-demo1"}}